---
title: "Example: jobs by public transport in Helsinki"
description: >
  A city outside Brazil, from open data: the streets of the Helsinki
  capital region from OpenStreetMap, HSL's timetables, and HSY's 250 m
  grids of residents and jobs; access to jobs at the morning peak,
  isochrones from the Central Station, and the options for one trip.
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
# This article is precomputed: its source is helsinki.Rmd.orig, knitted into
# helsinki.Rmd by `bash site/figures/make.sh --scenarios` (the data takes
# about 880 MB of downloads and osmium). Only the chunks written out below by
# cat() run when pkgdown renders it.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", error = FALSE)   # an error stops the knit
work <- file.path(tempdir(), "helsinki")
dir.create(work, showWarnings = FALSE)
if (nzchar(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_SCENARIOS_CACHE")) && !file.exists(file.path(work, "example-data"))) {
  file.symlink(Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_SCENARIOS_CACHE"), file.path(work, "example-data"))
}
knitr::opts_knit$set(root.dir = work)
local({
  hook <- knitr::knit_hooks$get("output")
  real <- normalizePath(file.path(work, "example-data"), mustWork = FALSE)
  knitr::knit_hooks$set(output = function(x, options) hook(gsub(real, "example-data", x, fixed = TRUE), options))
})
live <- function(code, opts = "echo = FALSE") {
  cat("```{r, ", opts, "}\n", code, "\n```\n", sep = "")
}
```

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('source("setup.R")', "include = FALSE")
```

peabiru reads any OpenStreetMap extract and any valid GTFS feed; nothing in
it is particular to Brazil. This example builds the network of the
Helsinki capital region (Helsinki, Espoo, Vantaa and Kauniainen, 1.25
million residents on HSY's grid) from open data, and measures access to jobs by public
transport at the morning peak.

**Why Helsinki.** The city abroad was chosen by its data: one official
GTFS feed for every mode of the region (HSL's buses, trams, metro,
commuter trains and ferries), current, under an explicit open licence (CC
BY 4.0); and residents *and*
jobs on a 250 m grid, also under CC BY 4.0, from the region's own
environmental services authority (HSY). Of the other candidates, Lisbon
has hills that would have made an elevation example, but the current
feeds of its core, Metro de Lisboa's and Carris's, are listed without a
licence in the Mobility Database catalogue (as downloaded on 2026-11-12;
the catalogue gives CC0 only for older copies, now deprecated, from the
Transporlis portal), so it was dropped rather than used on an assumption.
Bogotá, Mexico City and Montréal were not examined once Helsinki met
every requirement. Helsinki's terrain is gentle, and this example models
no elevation.

The output shown is what the code printed; it was computed on 2026-11-12.
The same steps in Python are in the [Python
example](../../python/example-helsinki/).

## The data

| Data | Source | Licence |
|---|---|---|
| Streets | [OpenStreetMap](https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright), extract of Finland by [Geofabrik](https://download.geofabrik.de/europe/finland.html), dated 2026-11-11 (776 MB, MD5 `ff7c5c52ce53880611989d15378b13af`), cut with osmium to the box 24.45–25.30° E, 60.03–60.42° N | ODbL 1.0 |
| Public transport | [HSL](https://www.hsl.fi/en/opendata) (Helsinki Region Transport), GTFS feed `https://infopalvelut.storage.hsldev.com/gtfs/hsl.zip`, published 2026-11-12 05:42 (feed_version), valid 2026-11-11 to 2027-01-09, downloaded on 2026-11-12 (88 MB, SHA-256 `1cd6f532…7223c`); the feed is replaced every day | CC BY 4.0 (HSL's open data terms); © HSL 2026 |
| Residents | HSY (Helsinki Region Environmental Services), [*Väestötietoruudukko*](https://www.hsy.fi/en/environmental-information/open-data/avoin-data---sivut/population-grid-of-helsinki-metropolitan-area/) 2025, residents on a 250 m grid (HSY's page of the dataset: updated 2026-07-08), from HSY's open WFS `kartta.hsy.fi` | CC BY 4.0, © HSY |
| Jobs | HSY, [*Työpaikkaruudukko*](https://www.hsy.fi/en/environmental-information/open-data/avoin-data---sivut/grid-data-on-enterprises-and-establishments-in-the-helsinki-region/) 2024, jobs (full-time equivalents) on a 250 m grid (updated 2026-08-26), same WFS | CC BY 4.0, © HSY; original source: Statistics Finland's Business Register |

Three things to know about the grids, each visible in the numbers below.

- **Residents.** Cells with fewer than five residents are left out of
  HSY's population grid. One of its 5,826 cells is not a place: HSY puts
  the residents it cannot assign to a building in a cell in the sea, at
  the south-east corner of the grid, and in 2025 that cell holds 33,575
  people, 2.6% of the total. It is left out here; routed as an origin it
  would count as 33,575 residents who reach no job.
- **Jobs.** The job grid counts jobs in companies and non-profit
  organisations only, **not public-sector jobs** (schools, hospitals, the
  administration), as full-time equivalents, and gives `-1` for cells
  with three or fewer employees, which are counted as 0 here. So "jobs"
  below are private and third-sector jobs, a lower bound.
- **Coverage.** The job grid covers the wider Helsinki region,
  572,915 jobs; the box of this example keeps the capital region,
  486,976 of them. Of the rest, 43,423 are in the outer municipalities and
  42,516 are in cells in the sea, south of the box (near 59.94° N,
  25.23° E), where HSY puts the establishments without a location.

```{r download, message = FALSE}
library(peabiru)
library(sf)

root <- Sys.getenv("PEABIRU_EXAMPLE_DATA", "example-data")
raw <- file.path(root, "raw")
data_dir <- file.path(root, "helsinki")
dir.create(raw, recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
options(timeout = 3600)   # the extract of Finland is 776 MB

fetch <- function(url, name = basename(url)) {
  dest <- file.path(raw, name)
  if (!file.exists(dest)) download.file(url, dest, mode = "wb", quiet = TRUE)
  dest
}
finland <- fetch("https://download.geofabrik.de/europe/finland-261111.osm.pbf")
stopifnot(tools::md5sum(finland) == "ff7c5c52ce53880611989d15378b13af")
hsl <- fetch("https://infopalvelut.storage.hsldev.com/gtfs/hsl.zip")
hsy <- paste0("https://kartta.hsy.fi/geoserver/wfs?service=WFS&version=2.0.0",
              "&request=GetFeature&outputFormat=application/json&srsName=EPSG:4326",
              "&typeNames=asuminen_ja_maankaytto:")
residents_file <- fetch(paste0(hsy, "Vaestotietoruudukko_2025"), "Vaestotietoruudukko_2025.geojson")
jobs_file <- fetch(paste0(hsy, "Tyopaikkaruudukko_2024"), "Tyopaikkaruudukko_2024.geojson")
```

## The network

The streets: the capital region and a margin, cut from the extract of
Finland. The timetables: HSL's feed holds two months of service, and its
`stop_times.txt` alone is 1.1 GB uncompressed. Routing one weekday morning
needs the trips of that day only, so the feed is cut to the trips that run
on Tuesday 17 November 2026 and the rows of the other tables they use. The
cut copies those rows unchanged (it changes no value), keeps every stop,
and leaves out the tables peabiru does not read (translations, areas):
in the hours analysed the network routes that day as one built from the
whole feed would (that equality was not run here: the whole feed was not
loaded; trips of Monday's service still running after midnight are not in
the cut), with 681,718 rows of `stop_times.txt` instead of two months of
them.

```{r network}
dir.create(file.path(data_dir, "network"), recursive = TRUE, showWarnings = FALSE)
status <- system2("osmium", c("extract", "--overwrite", "-s", "smart",
                              "-b", "24.45,60.03,25.30,60.42", finland,
                              "-o", file.path(data_dir, "network", "helsinki.osm.pbf")))
stopifnot(status == 0)

# the trips of a GTFS feed that run on `date`, and the rows of the other
# tables that belong to them, copied line by line
gtfs_one_day <- function(src, date, out) {
  day <- c("sunday", "monday", "tuesday", "wednesday", "thursday", "friday",
           "saturday")[as.POSIXlt(as.Date(date, "%Y%m%d"))$wday + 1]
  table <- function(name) read.csv(unz(src, name), colClasses = "character",
                                   check.names = FALSE, fileEncoding = "UTF-8-BOM")
  cal <- table("calendar.txt")
  active <- cal$service_id[cal$start_date <= date & cal$end_date >= date & cal[[day]] == "1"]
  cd <- table("calendar_dates.txt")
  cd <- cd[cd$date == date, ]
  active <- union(setdiff(active, cd$service_id[cd$exception_type == "2"]),
                  cd$service_id[cd$exception_type == "1"])
  trips <- table("trips.txt")
  trips <- trips[trips$service_id %in% active, ]
  keep <- list(trips.txt = c("trip_id", trips$trip_id), stop_times.txt = c("trip_id", trips$trip_id),
               routes.txt = c("route_id", unique(trips$route_id)),
               shapes.txt = c("shape_id", unique(trips$shape_id)),
               calendar.txt = c("service_id", active), calendar_dates.txt = c("service_id", active))
  tables <- intersect(c("agency.txt", "stops.txt", "routes.txt", "trips.txt", "stop_times.txt",
                        "calendar.txt", "calendar_dates.txt", "frequencies.txt", "transfers.txt",
                        "shapes.txt", "feed_info.txt", "fare_attributes.txt", "fare_rules.txt"),
                      unzip(src, list = TRUE)$Name)
  tmp <- tempfile()
  dir.create(tmp)
  for (name in tables) {
    con <- unz(src, name, open = "rb")
    to <- file(file.path(tmp, name), "wb")
    header <- readLines(con, n = 1)
    writeLines(header, to)
    k <- keep[[name]]
    col <- if (is.null(k)) NA else match(k[1], strsplit(sub("^﻿", "", sub("\r$", "", header)), ",")[[1]])
    repeat {
      lines <- readLines(con, n = 500000)
      if (length(lines) == 0) break
      if (!is.na(col)) {
        key <- read.csv(text = lines, header = FALSE, colClasses = "character")[[col]]
        stopifnot(length(key) == length(lines))   # no line breaks inside fields
        lines <- lines[key %in% k[-1]]
      }
      writeLines(lines, to)
    }
    close(con)
    close(to)
  }
  out <- file.path(normalizePath(dirname(out)), basename(out))
  unlink(out)
  old <- setwd(tmp)
  on.exit(setwd(old))
  stopifnot(zip(out, tables, flags = "-q9X") == 0)
  c(services = length(active), trips = nrow(trips))
}
gtfs_one_day(hsl, "20261117", file.path(data_dir, "network", "hsl.zip"))

get_gtfs_errors(file.path(data_dir, "network"))
threads <- 4   # of this laptop's ten cores
build_time <- system.time(
  network <- build_network(file.path(data_dir, "network"), n_threads = threads)
)[["elapsed"]]
network
build_time
```

The cut feed passes peabiru's GTFS checks without a
single error (the whole feed was not checked). Building the network took
`r round(build_time)` seconds here, with four threads on a laptop with
other work running (the timings on this page are indications); the R
process that knitted this page, from the cut of the feed to the last
table, peaked at 3.9 GB of memory.

## Residents and jobs

Each 250 m cell is placed at its centre; the residents are the origins,
the jobs the destinations.

```{r grids, warning = FALSE}
centres <- function(x) {
  st_coordinates(st_transform(st_centroid(st_geometry(st_transform(x, 3067))), 4326))
}
residents <- st_read(residents_file, quiet = TRUE)
xy <- centres(residents)
cells <- data.frame(id = as.character(residents$index), lon = round(xy[, 1], 6),
                    lat = round(xy[, 2], 6), population = residents$asukkaita)
# the cell in the sea with the residents without a building
sea <- which.max(cells$population)
stopifnot(cells$lat[sea] == min(cells$lat), cells$lon[sea] == max(cells$lon))
cells[sea, ]
cells <- cells[-sea, ]

jobs <- st_read(jobs_file, quiet = TRUE)
xy <- centres(jobs)
jobs <- data.frame(id = jobs$id, lon = round(xy[, 1], 6), lat = round(xy[, 2], 6),
                   jobs = pmax(jobs$tphklkm, 0), suppressed = jobs$tphklkm < 0)
c(jobs_in_the_grid = sum(jobs$jobs), without_a_location = sum(jobs$jobs[jobs$lat < 60]))
jobs <- jobs[jobs$lon > 24.45 & jobs$lon < 25.30 & jobs$lat > 60.03 & jobs$lat < 60.42, ]
c(cells = nrow(cells), residents = sum(cells$population),
  job_cells = nrow(jobs), jobs = sum(jobs$jobs), suppressed_job_cells = sum(jobs$suppressed))
```

## Jobs within reach at the morning peak

Walking and public transport, departures every minute from 07:30 to 08:30
on Tuesday 17 November 2026, the median travel time of each pair:

```{r accessibility}
peak <- as.POSIXct("2026-11-17 07:30:00", tz = "Europe/Helsinki")
t <- system.time(
  access <- accessibility(network, cells, jobs, opportunities_colnames = "jobs",
                          mode = c("WALK", "TRANSIT"), departure_datetime = peak,
                          time_window = 60, cutoffs = c(30, 45), n_threads = threads)
)
t[["elapsed"]]
access$population <- cells$population[match(access$id, cells$id)]
total <- sum(jobs$jobs)
sapply(split(access, access$cutoff), function(a) c(
  average_jobs = round(weighted.mean(a$accessibility, a$population)),
  average_share = round(weighted.mean(a$accessibility, a$population) / total, 3),
  residents_reaching_half = round(sum(a$population[a$accessibility >= total / 2]) / sum(a$population), 3)
))
```

`r format(nrow(cells), big.mark = ",")` origins, `r format(nrow(jobs), big.mark = ",")`
destinations and 60 departure minutes take about `r round(t[["elapsed"]])`
seconds.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("helsinki-jobs.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "A map of the Helsinki capital region covered by 250 m cells coloured by the number of jobs reachable within 45 minutes by public transport at the morning peak: the centre of Helsinki and the cells along the metro and railway lines reach the most."')
```

## Isochrones from the Central Station

```{r isochrones, message = FALSE}
station <- data.frame(id = "Helsinki Central Station", lon = 24.94124, lat = 60.17128)
s2 <- sf_use_s2(FALSE)   # see the note below
iso <- do.call(rbind, lapply(list("WALK", "BICYCLE", "CAR", c("WALK", "TRANSIT")), function(m) {
  i <- isochrone(network, station, mode = m, cutoffs = c(15, 30, 45), zoom = 10,
                 departure_datetime = peak, time_window = 60, n_threads = threads)
  data.frame(mode = paste(m, collapse = "+"), minutes = i$isochrone,
             km2 = round(as.numeric(st_area(st_transform(i, 3067))) / 1e6, 1))
}))
sf_use_s2(s2)
xtabs(km2 ~ mode + minutes, data = iso)
```

Driving is free flow: no congestion, so its isochrones are an upper bound
at the morning peak. The 45-minute areas by car and by public transport
run past the box cut from OpenStreetMap, where the network ends.

Why `sf_use_s2(FALSE)`: `isochrone()` passes each polygon through
`sf::st_make_valid()`, and that repair depends on whether sf
is using spherical geometry (s2, its default). For eleven of these twelve
polygons the area is the same either way; the 30-minute driving polygon,
which has 114 parts and 217 holes, comes out of the s2 repair at 523.8 km²
instead of 478.3, more than its outer rings enclose. With s2 off, the
areas are those of the bands as contoured, and those of the Python
example. With s2 on, `isochrone()` notices that the repair changed the
area, repairs that origin's polygons on the plane and warns (see
`?isochrone`), so here switching s2 off only avoids the warning.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
live('fig("helsinki-isochrones.svg")', 'echo = FALSE, fig.alt = "Four maps of the Helsinki capital region with the areas reached from Helsinki Central Station within 15, 30 and 45 minutes walking, cycling, driving and by public transport."')
```

## The options for one trip

From Itäkeskus, in the east, to the Aalto University campus at Otaniemi,
in Espoo: the trips worth considering by travel time, number of transfers
and walking time, leaving between 07:30 and 07:40.

```{r trip-options}
pair <- data.frame(id = c("Itäkeskus", "Otaniemi"), lon = c(25.0815, 24.8268),
                   lat = c(60.2102, 60.1847))
opts <- trip_options(network, pair[1, ], pair[2, ], departure_datetime = peak,
                     criteria = c("TIME", "TRANSFERS", "WALK"), itineraries = FALSE)
as.data.frame(opts$options[, c("option", "total_time", "transfers", "walk_time",
                               "departure_time", "description")])
```

The metro takes 39 minutes without a transfer, with a departure on each of
its two lines in those ten minutes; the two options that start by bus
take half an hour longer and are listed only because they walk a little
less.

## Variations

- **The whole feed.** On a machine with memory to spare, put HSL's feed in
  the network directory as it is: the routing on the chosen day should
  be the same (not run here).
- **Another region.** Any region with a valid GTFS feed and an
  OpenStreetMap extract works the same way (check the feed's licence);
  the HSY grids cover the Helsinki region only.
- **Scenarios.** The what-if arguments of the [Porto Alegre
  example](scenarios-porto-alegre.html) work here too, for instance
  `new_lts` on a planned cycle route.

